one is the complete zone the service lives in: ",[269,1110,542],{}," and ",[269,1113,1114],{},"defaultZone"," apply to ",[269,1117,271],{}," classes only. Registering the same identifier twice on one client is a no-op, and ",[269,1120,1102],{}," ends any stream the service is still producing with an error to its caller.",[277,1123,1125],{"id":1124},"the-service-directory","The service directory",[265,1127,1128,1129,1132,1133,1111,1136,1139,1140,1143,1144,1147,1148,1150,1151,1154,1155,1158,1159,1162,1163,1165],{},"Every published class under the project's ",[269,1130,1131],{},"servicesPaths"," gets a service definition from ",[269,1134,1135],{},"kinotic generate",[269,1137,1138],{},"kinotic sync",", its functions with their parameters and types, written to the ",[269,1141,1142],{},"ServiceDefinitions"," module under ",[269,1145,1146],{},"generatedPath",". A project that imports that module has the runtime register each service in the platform's service directory as the service comes online: the services registered before ",[269,1149,959],{}," when the client connects, and a service registered on a connected client as it registers. Only a service in an application's zone, ",[269,1152,1153],{},"app.\u003CorganizationId>.\u003CapplicationId>"," or a zone under it, is registered, through the platform's ",[269,1156,1157],{},"ApplicationServiceDirectory"," in ",[269,1160,1161],{},"app-api",", which refuses a zone that is not the application's or an application outside the caller's organization. The directory then holds the application's services beside the platform's own, with their liveness; a service the project declares no definition for serves on its zone alone. A definition naming other functions than the class serves fails the registration, and with it the connection, until ",[269,1164,1138],{}," runs again.",[1167,1168,1170],"h3",{"id":1169},"parameter-and-return-types","Parameter and return types",[265,1172,1173],{},"Arguments and results travel as JSON, so a function's types are published as JSON carries them:",[1175,1176,1177,1193,1203,1224,1233,1246],"ul",{},[1178,1179,1180,1181,1184,1185,1188,1189,1192],"li",{},"A ",[269,1182,1183],{},"Promise"," or ",[269,1186,1187],{},"PromiseLike"," result is asynchronous and an ",[269,1190,1191],{},"Observable"," result is a stream, of the type it resolves to or emits.",[1178,1194,1195,1198,1199,1202],{},[269,1196,1197],{},"any",", ",[269,1200,1201],{},"unknown",", and a type parameter with no constraint accept anything; a constrained type parameter is its constraint.",[1178,1204,1205,1206,1209,1210,1111,1213,1216,1217,1198,1220,1223],{},"Interfaces, classes (their fields, without their methods), generic instantiations such as ",[269,1207,1208],{},"Page\u003CReport>",", mapped types such as ",[269,1211,1212],{},"Partial\u003CReport>",[269,1214,1215],{},"Pick\u003CReport, 'id'>",", intersections, and unions of objects are objects, named for the type and its type arguments (",[269,1218,1219],{},"Page_Report",[269,1221,1222],{},"Partial_Report",").",[1178,1225,1226,1111,1229,1232],{},[269,1227,1228],{},"Record\u003Cstring, V>",[269,1230,1231],{},"{[key: string]: V}"," are maps, enums are enums, and a union of string literals is a string.",[1178,1234,1180,1235,1238,1239,1241,1242,1245],{},[269,1236,1237],{},"Date"," is published as a date, but it arrives as the ISO-8601 string a date travels as: the service receives a string, and a TypeScript caller gets a string back. ",[269,1240,1135],{}," logs a warning for each one, and the directory carries the same warning in the ",[269,1243,1244],{},"warnings"," metadata of the parameter, or of the function for a result.",[1178,1247,1180,1248,1198,1251,1198,1254,1198,1257,1198,1260,1263],{},[269,1249,1250],{},"Map",[269,1252,1253],{},"Set",[269,1255,1256],{},"Uint8Array",[269,1258,1259],{},"Buffer",[269,1261,1262],{},"bigint",", tuple, or function fails generation with the reason and the type to declare instead.",[277,1265,1267],{"id":1266},"decorators","Decorators",[1167,1269,1271],{"id":1270},"publishnamespace-name-advertise","@Publish(namespace?, name?, advertise?)",[265,1273,1274,1275,1278,1279,1282,1283,1286,1287,1290,1291,1294],{},"Marks a class for publication. The full service identifier becomes ",[269,1276,1277],{},"namespace.ClassName","; both parts are optional (the name defaults to the class name). A service published with no namespace is addressed by its name in its zone, ",[269,1280,1281],{},"srv:\u002F\u002F\u003Czone>~ClassName",", and the platform's service directory names it in its zone's namespace, ",[269,1284,1285],{},"\u003Czone>.ClassName",", so services of the same name in different applications stay apart. Passing ",[269,1288,1289],{},"advertise"," as ",[269,1292,1293],{},"true"," additionally advertises the service in the platform service directory, so it appears in directory listings for browsing and invocation — publishing alone makes a service callable over RPC without listing it.",[1167,1296,1298],{"id":1297},"zonezone","@Zone(zone)",[265,1300,1301,1302,1304],{},"Declares the zone the service is addressable in, relative to the client's ",[269,1303,542],{},". The zone is one or more dot-separated labels of lowercase letters, digits, and interior dashes.",[1167,1306,1308],{"id":1307},"versionversion","@Version(version)",[265,1310,1311,1312,1315],{},"Sets the semantic version for the service in ",[269,1313,1314],{},"X.Y.Z"," format. Callers can pin to a specific version when resolving the service.",[1167,1317,1319],{"id":1318},"scope","@Scope",[265,1321,1322],{},"A getter or method decorator that marks the member providing the scope identifier. Scope targets requests at one specific instance of a service — for example the copy running on a particular node or device.",[282,1324,1326],{"className":284,"code":1325,"language":286,"meta":287,"style":287},"import { Publish, Scope } from '@kinotic-ai\u002Fcore'\n\n@Publish('com.example')\nclass DeviceService {\n    deviceId: string\n\n    @